<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Sayn</h1><p class="st-meta">1139 CE – 1606 CE (approx.) · capital Sayn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Westerwald county near the Rhine whose counts, at their height in the 13th century, rivalled the archbishops of Cologne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Sayn appear in 1139 with a castle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bendorf">Bendorf</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-count-of-sayn">Henry III of Sayn</a>, who ruled until 1247, built up a large territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegerland">Siegerland</a>, and was accused of heresy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-of-marburg">Konrad of Marburg</a> in 1233 but cleared at an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diet</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After Henry III's death the lands passed through his sister to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sponheim">Sponheim</a> family, forming Sayn and Sayn-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a> lines. The counts ruled hilly lands with iron mines and abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayn-abbey">Sayn Abbey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienstatt-abbey">Marienstatt</a>. They adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the death of Count Henry IV in 1606 the main line ended, and the county was divided after long disputes between heirs and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Archbishopric of Trier</a>. Sayn-Hachenburg and Sayn-Altenkirchen later passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The Sayn-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a> princes survive.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-sayn.jpg" alt="County of Sayn" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Nikephoros; derivative work by Beckstet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1450_Sayn_Map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1139 CE – 1606 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sayn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry III of Sayn, Henry IV of Sayn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Moselle Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sayn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=county-of-sayn" aria-label="County of Sayn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4853 426 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Sayn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5173 682L5229 694M5173 682L5191 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5191 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5173 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5173 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sayn. <a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=county-of-sayn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
